// #taxon[Taxon]
#title[Thoughts on another definition for the dirichlet type]

Update: The following definition is much closer to what Baez describes in week300

In some cases, such as with the #link("00EC")[zeta species] $Z$, a $Z$ structure already gives us a group of the correct order.
$
  D_F (S) :-= sum_(A:"FinSet"), sum_(R: Z(A) ) S -> T(G(R)),
$
where $G(R)$ is the underlying additive group of the Ring, and $T(G)$ the type of G-torsors.
Baez calls the inner sigma type a twisted product, because of automorphisms.
The #link("00D6")[symmetry book] has a section on $G$-torsors. $T(G(R))$ is equivalent to the type $B G(R)$.
